  "live_records": {
    "q-59898": {
      "dom_id": "step-q59898",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14201980/assets/ec8c3585db1981d001864b3c135f84e46643051d22f28e77f82e8b5a92d0f09d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ squares for every $1$ circle.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $3$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of squares to circles is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:1$ because there is $1$ square for every $1$ circle.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
